#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203684 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203919 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "220647d1-71b8-5d0a-b497-e5d3ad7b6da2" :knowledge-point/title "Representing Binary Relations Using Graphs"}] :question/id #uuid "53571345-258b-54e1-af9c-fa68d0d0f40e" :question/math-academy-id "e-14613" :question/problem "Let $A = \\{ 4, 6, 12 \\}.$ Construct a graph for the following relation:\n\n$$\nR = \\{ (x,y) \\in A^2 \\::\\: x \\mid y\\}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "A binary relation can be visualized using a plot (or graph), where \n\n- the first set is plotted along the horizontal axis,\n\n- the second set is plotted along the vertical axis, and\n\n- each pair $(x,y)$ from the relation defines a point with coordinates $(x,y)$ on the plot.\n\nThe relation $R$ is the set of ordered pairs $(x,y) \\in A^2$ with $x \\mid y.$\n\n- If $x = 4,$ then the values $y = 4, 12$ satisfy $x \\mid y.$ So, $R$ contains the ordered pairs $(4,4)$ and $(4,12),$ and we should plot these points on the graph.\n\n- If $x = 6,$ then the values $y = 6, 12$ satisfy $x \\mid y.$ So, $R$ contains the ordered pairs $(6,6)$ and $(6,12),$ and we should plot these points on the graph.\n\n- If $x = 12,$ then the value $y = 12$ satisfies $x \\mid y.$ So, $R$ contains the ordered pair $(12,12),$ and we should plot this point on the graph.\n\nTherefore, we have the following plot:\n\n![](images/48/48836a0157b46f3f7af3ceb0db094612794b7da6c2d749bd8769f87da410b0d6.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203685 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203920 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "65e9c4fd-dae7-5858-b008-ca0e144fa64a" :knowledge-point/title "Representing Binary Relations Using Digraphs"}] :question/id #uuid "5c1451cc-7b5c-5f8f-9dfb-60c18319bce8" :question/math-academy-id "e-14614" :question/problem "![](images/9d/9d07b5e999e4f0c9f403bf5ac3d61dfd12e1accd11a72d68a2f3b39196a58060.png)\n\n \n\nThe directed graph of a relation defined on the set $\\{ 1, 3, 5 \\}$ is given above. Which of the following ordered pairs belong to the relation?\n\nI. $(1, 5)$\n\nII. $(5, 5)$\n\nIII. $(3, 1)$" :question/worked-solution "A binary relation can be visualized using a directed graph (digraph), where \n\n- the nodes (vertices) of the graph are elements of the underlying set,\n\n- each pair $(x,y)$ from the relation defines an arrow from node $x$ to node $y$,\n\n- each pair $(x,x)$ from the relation defines a loop from node $x$ to itself.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given ordered pairs.\n\n- The pair $(1, 5)$ belongs to the relation. Indeed, the directed graph has an arrow that starts at the node $1$ and ends at the node $5.$\n\n- The pair $(5, 5)$ belongs to the relation. Indeed, the directed graph has a loop from the node $5$ to itself.\n\n- The pair $(3, 1)$ does not belong to the relation since the directed graph does not have an arrow that starts at the node $3$ and ends at the node $1.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203698 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203918 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "08140eb8-479d-5827-9dee-d51befd7aadd" :knowledge-point/title "Representing Binary Relations Using Mapping Diagrams"}] :question/id #uuid "9306fe02-b1dd-584c-852f-27778aed9d8b" :question/math-academy-id "e-14650" :question/problem "Suppose we have the sets $A$ and $B,$ given by\n\n$$\nA=\\{1,2,3 \\}, \\qquad B=\\{-1,2,5\\}.\n$$\n\nConstruct a mapping diagram for the following relation:\n\n$$\nR= \\big\\{ (x,y) \\in A \\times B \\: : \\: x\\cdot y \\geq 4 \\big\\}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The ordered pairs belonging to our relation correspond to the arrows on the mapping diagram. Each arrow starts at the first component of the pair and ends at its second component.\n\nWe're given the following sets:\n\n$$\nA=\\{1,2,3 \\}, \\qquad B=\\{-1,2,5\\}.\n$$\n\nThe relation $R$ is the set of ordered pairs $(x,y) \\in A \\times B$ with $x\\cdot y \\geq 4.$\n\n- If $x=1,$ then the value $y=5$ satisfies $x\\cdot y \\geq 4.$ So, $R$ contains the ordered pair $(1,5),$ and we should add the arrow $1 \\rightarrow 5$ to the mapping diagram.\n\n- If $x=2,$ then the values $y=2$ and $y=5$ satisfy $x\\cdot y \\geq 4.$ So, $R$ contains the ordered pairs $(2,2)$ and $(2,5),$ and we should add the arrows $2 \\rightarrow 2$ and $2 \\rightarrow 5$ to the mapping diagram.\n\n- If $x=3,$ then the values $y=2$ and $y=5$ satisfy $x\\cdot y \\geq 4.$ So, $R$ contains the ordered pairs $(3,2)$ and $(3,5),$ and we should add the arrows $3 \\rightarrow 2$ and $3 \\rightarrow 5$ to the mapping diagram.\n\nTherefore, we have the following plot:\n\n![](images/7d/7ddf9a6fb23a475e75128b3c4bce145b5488eb5c12dfe592578cdf10435dc1a3.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204228 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203919 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "220647d1-71b8-5d0a-b497-e5d3ad7b6da2" :knowledge-point/title "Representing Binary Relations Using Graphs"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "29ccb1b7-2ca6-5dee-b540-bece0f9bdc86" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415998} {:answer/id #uuid "88b2d8a9-0488-5aa6-bb5a-cb908ed36229" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416019} {:answer/id #uuid "9c646536-2075-5ae1-8df0-abb2e9284492" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416020} {:answer/id #uuid "a89cc823-46c1-5241-b3c5-27d4cd4aae51" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416022} {:answer/id #uuid "d66daf91-eceb-5f72-b909-5358356f4844" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416031}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "29ccb1b7-2ca6-5dee-b540-bece0f9bdc86" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415998} :answer-field/id #uuid "1dc780e3-5a21-59ce-aa65-e72163175a31" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416049}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5e5c1c70-9337-53c9-94bd-f7f57b8f63c1" :question/math-academy-id "q-212112" :question/problem "![](images/01/015cb5b350ad2692c1371d20c367e6c01a81b981351ba33b61e6c95854862d63.png)\n\nThe plot of the binary relation $S,$ defined over the sets $X=\\{2,3,4\\}$ and $Y=\\{♣,♠\\},$ is given above. Which of the following ordered pairs belong to the relation?\n\nI. $(2,♠)$\n\nII. $(3,♣)$\n\nIII. $(4,♣)$" :question/worked-solution "A binary relation can be visualized using a plot (or graph), where \n\n- the first set is plotted along the horizontal axis,\n\n- the second set is plotted along the vertical axis, and\n\n- each pair $(x,y)$ from the relation defines a point with coordinates $(x,y)$ on the plot.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given ordered pairs.\n\n- The pair $(2,♠)$ belongs to the relation. Indeed, the plot contains a point with these coordinates.\n\n- The pair $(3,♣)$ does not belong to the relation since the plot does not contain a point with such coordinates.\n\n- The pair $(4,♣)$ belongs to the relation. Indeed, the plot contains a point with these coordinates.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186416070 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203919 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "220647d1-71b8-5d0a-b497-e5d3ad7b6da2" :knowledge-point/title "Representing Binary Relations Using Graphs"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0c6d623c-ebdd-5e72-8f57-388e3aa09b2c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/5e/5eb68dca550b17d13a752b44fcf6c026f58e266100210d31834a73b19f9ba6e9.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415987} {:answer/id #uuid "190f3569-4d1f-5902-8f57-3f9b901f5eb2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/4e/4e08efd6af5e38ed82c0c1659088620b673afbfad5a751c07a492dd0fc4c497c.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415993} {:answer/id #uuid "2b08bc91-d831-5725-b463-d06eeef20685" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/d2/d23ceda71423d8ce91cf96f110df8705bf425c9b823450d70548d94612103aec.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415999} {:answer/id #uuid "6962413b-7c51-50e9-9538-19bbb06c7019" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/a6/a6c5137b47544ab46146ec0f951442c1cb268ec19b4312d9364647f4148c5fad.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416012} {:answer/id #uuid "dccf3751-5b92-5a35-99dd-3f4174a3fc82" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/14/1495bb2a62a1b20082862f0a547a7d89a91a2dd6238d1452a8a72800733558b7.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416033}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "190f3569-4d1f-5902-8f57-3f9b901f5eb2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/4e/4e08efd6af5e38ed82c0c1659088620b673afbfad5a751c07a492dd0fc4c497c.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415993} :answer-field/id #uuid "b5d5380e-ae3a-5c0f-ac14-bf33cf3c6bb1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416055}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "af34d9f6-e221-5c78-a119-1fdc9bcb5c87" :question/math-academy-id "q-212114" :question/problem "Which of the following is the graph representing the relation $R=\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}<81\\}?$" :question/worked-solution "A binary relation can be visualized using a plot (or graph), where \n\n- the first set goes along the horizontal line,\n\n- the second set goes along the vertical line,\n\n- each pair $(x,y)$ from the relation defines a point with coordinates $(x,y)$ on the plot.\n\nIn our case, the plot contains all the points in ${R}^{2}$ for which \n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}<81.\n$$\n\nThese are the points that lie *inside* the circle of radius $9$ centered at the origin.\n\nFinally, since we have a strict inequality, the points on the circle must be excluded from the plot, which we depict by using a *dashed* curve:\n\n![](images/4e/4e08efd6af5e38ed82c0c1659088620b673afbfad5a751c07a492dd0fc4c497c.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204230 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203920 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "65e9c4fd-dae7-5858-b008-ca0e144fa64a" :knowledge-point/title "Representing Binary Relations Using Digraphs"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0e527545-c8a4-5706-a526-a45e5bdba53b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415989} {:answer/id #uuid "aae26d10-6696-5509-93bb-3f2a97889821" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416024} {:answer/id #uuid "bd0608ce-eb8d-5612-b223-1f6931919f8c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416028} {:answer/id #uuid "c8d15f20-fd7b-5b6b-a3b1-2360e322ea86" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416029} {:answer/id #uuid "e877ff7a-c423-5005-80f4-b878e8065f4b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416038}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c8d15f20-fd7b-5b6b-a3b1-2360e322ea86" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416029} :answer-field/id #uuid "001f212a-2c89-570f-a93d-35b3d3dd68fe" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416045}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "fc05772e-ea0c-5a4b-9ddc-8e7c8974bf6f" :question/math-academy-id "q-212120" :question/problem "![](images/84/847bcee3b9f9fc6672d5858111866141e043e390605cd92fb5579fd5193d7df4.png)\n\nThe directed graph of a relation defined on the set $\\{1,2,3\\}$ is given above. Which of the following ordered pairs belong to the relation?\n\nI. $(2,1)$\n\nII. $(3,3)$\n\nIII. $(2,3)$" :question/worked-solution "A binary relation can be visualized using a directed graph (digraph), where \n\n- the nodes (vertices) of the graph are elements of the underlying set,\n\n- each pair $(x,y)$ from the relation defines an arrow from node $x$ to node $y$,\n\n- each pair $(x,x)$ from the relation defines a loop from node $x$ to itself.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given ordered pairs.\n\n- The pair $(2,1)$ belongs to the relation. Indeed, the directed graph has an arrow that starts at the node $2$ and ends at the node $1.$\n\n- The pair $(3,3)$ belongs to the relation. Indeed, the directed graph has a loop from the node $3$ to itself.\n\n- The pair $(2,3)$ does not belong to the relation since the directed graph does not have an arrow that starts at the node $2$ and ends at the node $3.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204249 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203918 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "08140eb8-479d-5827-9dee-d51befd7aadd" :knowledge-point/title "Representing Binary Relations Using Mapping Diagrams"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "87a5ae30-ae4a-5f92-85a7-a2d709a9af1d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/bf/bf7cde9088d0559ea2732883b38763691f42c706a4f68b51bd0db14d3cfcf711.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186200201} {:answer/id #uuid "94e867fb-4620-5583-b02d-b1726ccc7bac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/d6/d66cabe448b2b33804721dd3cfa884de858a5e4fa6056991f612465070d54da5.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186200202} {:answer/id #uuid "b33ae6a6-a514-5803-b56d-5904be3bbbc5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/d5/d5e67fe868b8abe416b3e41664baac5f84c84035615ee5dd4d9d010a6ec44132.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186200203} {:answer/id #uuid "223352b6-e4f2-5d15-8550-bc8a14fcd924" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/85/85ca1d890803d6a8e3a264f99e20139bbac1217d7009280db5f01f8d8d18503a.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186200204} {:answer/id #uuid "5569f83c-8129-5e08-9ea4-193d0af16b9e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/c8/c8959920a0b91ec210049df92127769c80ebd17a1614df02480c3b84473f41db.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186200205}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "94e867fb-4620-5583-b02d-b1726ccc7bac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/d6/d66cabe448b2b33804721dd3cfa884de858a5e4fa6056991f612465070d54da5.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186200202} :answer-field/id #uuid "522a5b45-852a-5bb2-98ea-8399dc5fc78a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186200200}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "59914551-baaa-5606-8583-0693c5e13bd6" :question/math-academy-id "q-213011" :question/problem "Suppose we have the sets $A$ and $B,$ given by\n\n$$\nA=\\{0.5,2,\\sqrt{5}\\},\\,B=\\{-1,0,1\\}.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following mapping diagrams represents the following relation? \n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y)\\in A\\times B\\,:\\,{x}^{y}\\in Z\\}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The ordered pairs belonging to our relation correspond to the arrows on the mapping diagram. Each arrow starts at the first component of the pair and ends at its second component.\n\nWe're given the following sets:\n\n$$\nA=\\{0.5,2,\\sqrt{5}\\},\\,B=\\{-1,0,1\\}.\n$$\n\nThe relation $R$ is the set of ordered pairs $(x,y)\\in A\\times B$ with ${x}^{y}\\in Z.$\n\n- If $x=0.5,$ then the values $y=-1,0$ satisfy ${x}^{y}\\in Z.$ So, $R$ contains the ordered pairs $(0.5,-1)$ and $(0.5,0),$ and we should add the arrows $0.5\\to -1$ and $0.5\\to 0$ to the mapping diagram.\n\n- If $x=2,$ then the values $y=0,1$ satisfy ${x}^{y}\\in Z.$ So, $R$ contains the ordered pairs $(2,0)$ and $(2,1),$ and we should add the arrows $2\\to 0$ and $2\\to 1$ to the mapping diagram.\n\n- If $x=\\sqrt{5},$ then the value $y=0$ satisfies ${x}^{y}\\in Z.$ So, $R$ contains the ordered pair $(\\sqrt{5},0),$ and we should add the arrow $\\sqrt{5}\\to 0$ to the mapping diagram.\n\nTherefore, we have the following plot:\n\n![](images/d6/d66cabe448b2b33804721dd3cfa884de858a5e4fa6056991f612465070d54da5.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186416072 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203919 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "220647d1-71b8-5d0a-b497-e5d3ad7b6da2" :knowledge-point/title "Representing Binary Relations Using Graphs"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "13272a5b-2de1-5012-9d4c-d0b6e9760b36" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415992} {:answer/id #uuid "26c9b0be-0411-58d9-acc8-3ddc5b99d9ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415997} {:answer/id #uuid "38c8fc2e-e74c-5869-91a3-f1b2979621f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416002} {:answer/id #uuid "6905a4c0-8040-5b6a-87f7-803e19676be0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416011} {:answer/id #uuid "edbf0707-8642-500c-bb4f-336aa28e52ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416039}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "38c8fc2e-e74c-5869-91a3-f1b2979621f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416002} :answer-field/id #uuid "ca8636af-9023-557c-9e61-5c98ec22e1da" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416059}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e27df10f-2205-5c33-84a9-013636e28935" :question/math-academy-id "q-213167" :question/problem "![](images/35/35756183a7b4bf08baffb00d1f35dcc370b0e58831554daafa58d8f3d4359ab6.png)\n\nThe plot of the binary relation $S,$ defined over the set $X=\\{2,4,6\\}$ is given above. Which of the following ordered pairs belong to the relation?\n\nI. $(2,2)$\n\nII. $(4,4)$\n\nIII. $(6,2)$" :question/worked-solution "A binary relation can be visualized using a plot (or graph), where \n\n- the first set is plotted along the horizontal axis,\n\n- the second set is plotted along the vertical axis, and\n\n- each pair $(x,y)$ from the relation defines a point with coordinates $(x,y)$ on the plot.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given ordered pairs.\n\n- The pair $(2,2)$ belongs to the relation. Indeed, the plot contains a point with these coordinates.\n\n- The pair $(4,4)$ does not belong to the relation since the plot does not contain a point with such coordinates.\n\n- The pair $(6,2)$ belongs to the relation. Indeed, the plot contains a point with these coordinates.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186416062 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203919 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "220647d1-71b8-5d0a-b497-e5d3ad7b6da2" :knowledge-point/title "Representing Binary Relations Using Graphs"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "52a8bfcd-d6f1-5e95-a1b3-95f561da75f4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/59/59ada0e07a72956a300ac0de27e75ea8598d7f7d0ccc1e2b51fc31c127cf8f15.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416007} {:answer/id #uuid "6291cf00-36f4-5f85-9343-de7d394117ce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/3d/3dec67ac3a21ca9db5967f5e650fd79d6aaadab05c900db24b7a9669a872f2f2.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416010} {:answer/id #uuid "b4795e31-10f4-5cb3-b737-d0f8c02771c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/6e/6e3abe1d717f07230d9712db4a5006b9da27859760d20df4eafe6b7b5f24afc3.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416026} {:answer/id #uuid "da12c4e3-6b6f-543e-a71d-beed250594af" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/4d/4d08b577582567c0af86201b86ebd6b8a6a837cb0db91cbc047773f58017f3b8.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416032} {:answer/id #uuid "f7af45d2-f112-51b6-9f3b-2e3977b69236" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/fb/fb730add8167fcad4b03902d9cafbb0163f3ae00ee7d4c635d9c1d82211c3162.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416042}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6291cf00-36f4-5f85-9343-de7d394117ce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/3d/3dec67ac3a21ca9db5967f5e650fd79d6aaadab05c900db24b7a9669a872f2f2.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416010} :answer-field/id #uuid "e4c18e7a-0cb9-5985-b003-006e3604014c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416060}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "18473aee-7220-58ca-a3d1-b01636850f3f" :question/math-academy-id "q-213180" :question/problem "Let $A=\\{1,2,3\\}.$ Which of the following is the graph representing the relation $R=\\{(x,y)\\in {A}^{2}\\,:\\,x+y=4\\}?$" :question/worked-solution "A binary relation can be visualized using a plot (or graph), where \n\n- the first set is plotted along the horizontal axis,\n\n- the second set is plotted along the vertical axis, and\n\n- each pair $(x,y)$ from the relation defines a point with coordinates $(x,y)$ on the plot.\n\nThe relation $R$ is the set of ordered pairs $(x,y)\\in {A}^{2}$ with $x+y=4.$\n\n- If $x=1,$ then the value $y=3$ satisfies $x+y=4.$ So, $R$ contains the ordered pair $(1,3),$ and we should plot this point on the graph.\n\n- If $x=2,$ then the value $y=2$ satisfies $x+y=4.$ So, $R$ contains the ordered pair $(2,2),$ and we should plot this point on the graph.\n\n- If $x=3,$ then the value $y=1$ satisfies $x+y=4.$ So, $R$ contains the ordered pair $(3,1),$ and we should plot this point on the graph.\n\nTherefore, we have the following plot:\n\n![](images/3d/3dec67ac3a21ca9db5967f5e650fd79d6aaadab05c900db24b7a9669a872f2f2.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186416068 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203919 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "220647d1-71b8-5d0a-b497-e5d3ad7b6da2" :knowledge-point/title "Representing Binary Relations Using Graphs"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1b552ec2-8bae-5f6c-b902-514499f8d3ef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/42/42f68f95fe216faacbfaa051ec0a1a6cf75fe7e4598061e94f2ec6be87f9541f.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415995} {:answer/id #uuid "365f26d9-e9d3-55db-9fae-badc735ec9c6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/56/56d64fc3b536fe0ef88e7ac600d98a269167fd2039f8b3bcce586ae2a9b72ef5.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416001} {:answer/id #uuid "474bc48d-3b12-51f8-837e-1c568bd96891" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/a4/a4d1ba20a0d170c62380b80a037c316ed894ceb0af624a0591bfabdf7a0a036b.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416005} {:answer/id #uuid "8327d456-842b-5b16-abc5-17cbf70f9780" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/53/53e4726a02da9ba18322717180334849b93ce1cc15c59dcd22fa3e97b6fe8cfb.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416018} {:answer/id #uuid "aa4f4494-0c2f-514c-a137-c3a3d314a308" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/1d/1dfcdd95669026e2263465f71b67d4913fdfddee18138a8516487ed14d3e02c1.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416023}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1b552ec2-8bae-5f6c-b902-514499f8d3ef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/42/42f68f95fe216faacbfaa051ec0a1a6cf75fe7e4598061e94f2ec6be87f9541f.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415995} :answer-field/id #uuid "c0ba57a4-06ca-5eb1-846d-69ae94a3af23" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416057}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8c54244e-f11b-5241-a798-1237f1be29cb" :question/math-academy-id "q-213188" :question/problem "Let $A=\\{3,6,8\\}$ and $B=\\{1,4,7\\}$ Which of the following is the graph representing the relation $R=\\{(x,y)\\in A\\times B\\,:\\,x≡y\\,(\\text{mod}\\,2)\\}?$" :question/worked-solution "A binary relation can be visualized using a plot (or graph), where \n\n- the first set is plotted along the horizontal axis,\n\n- the second set is plotted along the vertical axis, and\n\n- each pair $(x,y)$ from the relation defines a point with coordinates $(x,y)$ on the plot.\n\nThe relation $R$ is the set of ordered pairs $(x,y)\\in A\\times B$ with $x≡y\\,(\\text{mod}\\,2).$\n\n- If $x=3,$ then the values $y=1,7$ satisfy $x≡y\\,(\\text{mod}\\,2).$ So, $R$ contains the ordered pairs $(3,1)$ and $(3,7),$ and we should plot these points on the graph.\n\n- If $x=6,$ then the value $y=4$ satisfies $x≡y\\,(\\text{mod}\\,2).$ So, $R$ contains the ordered pair $(6,4),$ and we should plot this point on the graph.\n\n- If $x=8,$ then the value $y=4$ satisfies $x≡y\\,(\\text{mod}\\,2).$ So, $R$ contains the ordered pair $(8,4),$ and we should plot this point on the graph.\n\nTherefore, we have the following plot:\n\n![](images/42/42f68f95fe216faacbfaa051ec0a1a6cf75fe7e4598061e94f2ec6be87f9541f.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186416066 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203918 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "08140eb8-479d-5827-9dee-d51befd7aadd" :knowledge-point/title "Representing Binary Relations Using Mapping Diagrams"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2fe08085-f3f9-5e2f-a722-3ea0e9c32a9b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "d" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416000}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2fe08085-f3f9-5e2f-a722-3ea0e9c32a9b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "d" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416000} :answer-field/id #uuid "1eee9d1d-feca-5d66-bf87-af9b0f120f4b" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416050} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0db9b435-693c-51f8-83dd-ff1f9738f23d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "c" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415988}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0db9b435-693c-51f8-83dd-ff1f9738f23d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "c" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415988} :answer-field/id #uuid "40d0464a-50e8-56be-98bb-0752d1bf1b9e" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416052} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "039c3002-4123-5354-a54b-3c284e8cbe22" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "b" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415984}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "039c3002-4123-5354-a54b-3c284e8cbe22" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "b" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415984} :answer-field/id #uuid "ed66a49d-bd94-53a1-92eb-bd214fe6b1b7" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416061}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "61dc2470-cb3c-5469-b0b2-8c0f8e6eb11f" :question/math-academy-id "q-213253" :question/problem "![](images/c2/c22d8afca77121e994303cc67c6e85b065b452a5725603dec04273d1c8d427b3.png)\n\nExpress the relation $R⊆A\\times A$ represented by the mapping diagram above as a set of ordered pairs.\n\n$R=\\{(a,b),(b,$\n{{field-1}}$),(c,a),($\n{{field-2}}$,d),($\n{{field-3}}$,b)\\}$" :question/worked-solution "The ordered pairs belonging to our relation correspond to the arrows on the mapping diagram. Each arrow starts at the first component of the pair and ends at its second component.\n\nOur diagram has the following arrows:\n\n- Arrows starting from $a:$\n\n- $a\\to b,$ so the relation contains the pair $(a,b).$\n\n- Arrows starting from $b:$\n\n- $b\\to b,$ so the relation contains the pair $(b,b).$\n\n- Arrows starting from $c:$\n\n- $c\\to a$ and $c\\to d,$ so the relation contains the pairs $(c,a)$ and $(c,d).$\n\n- Arrows starting from $d:$\n\n- $d\\to b,$ so the relation contains the pair $(d,b).$\n\nTherefore, $R$ contains the following ordered pairs:\n\n$$\nR=\\{(a,b),(b,b),(c,a),(c,d),(d,b)\\}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186416065 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203918 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "08140eb8-479d-5827-9dee-d51befd7aadd" :knowledge-point/title "Representing Binary Relations Using Mapping Diagrams"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "61e01a20-35e1-5a7a-9d7e-41618423ad8d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416009}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "61e01a20-35e1-5a7a-9d7e-41618423ad8d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416009} :answer-field/id #uuid "5dcbbc5c-d4a7-5b48-9988-d808410582cc" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416053} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3e9c041f-47e8-5bb5-8991-9df243d3ae3e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416004}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3e9c041f-47e8-5bb5-8991-9df243d3ae3e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416004} :answer-field/id #uuid "6247a186-33d8-5bdd-abab-82aa8c37f29b" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416054} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6a835395-6491-59ff-aba2-7fdd3ccb16ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416013}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6a835395-6491-59ff-aba2-7fdd3ccb16ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416013} :answer-field/id #uuid "c8604724-b466-5c49-9eac-6df36c526cb1" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416058}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "57a0e862-6493-53ed-a45b-3b4d586c00e7" :question/math-academy-id "q-213254" :question/problem "![](images/38/38df1bcf68146317ae6450e495658203a6427551d0510bd232c8cbfe5b82d386.png)\n\nExpress the relation $R⊆A\\times A$ represented by the mapping diagram above as a set of ordered pairs.\n\n$R=\\{(w,w),(w,$\n{{field-1}}$),($\n{{field-2}}$,x),(z,$\n{{field-3}}$)\\}$" :question/worked-solution "The ordered pairs belonging to our relation correspond to the arrows on the mapping diagram. Each arrow starts at the first component of the pair and ends at its second component.\n\nOur diagram has the following arrows:\n\n- Arrows starting from $w:$\n\n- $w\\to w$ and $w\\to z,$ so the relation contains the pairs $(w,w)$ and $(w,z).$\n\n- No arrows start from $x.$\n\n- Arrows starting from $y:$\n\n- $y\\to x,$ so the relation contains the pair $(y,x).$\n\n- Arrows starting from $z:$\n\n- $z\\to x,$ so the relation contains the pair $(z,x).$\n\nTherefore, $R$ contains the following ordered pairs:\n\n$$\nR=\\{(w,w),(w,z),(y,x),(z,x)\\}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186419024 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203918 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "08140eb8-479d-5827-9dee-d51befd7aadd" :knowledge-point/title "Representing Binary Relations Using Mapping Diagrams"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "45d65c90-dfbc-57d3-96ca-a717e1f395a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/90/90f18f9bc042dda952e423a56268bd6b917db2f94f9a7b42ee8087290b169fa4.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419018} {:answer/id #uuid "4b79f667-cd57-53d0-854e-fda5aee2821d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/cd/cd9e524895740aa64ee6143d3d600d6be38b7e6e71b00d402ee249c11c3da36e.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419019} {:answer/id #uuid "d174cbc8-3270-5fcf-b235-c7e8a0ccc0cc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/7b/7bac8457b66917fc1aa9866c04fddef3685196f48d68cd9ce40642b144cb41aa.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419020} {:answer/id #uuid "f24e9d57-53f5-569d-babd-8bd097337ac0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/3a/3a95797dc283876f7b5c4d71c1f954481cb5d21caa7a1c5acbdaec952991c84f.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419021} {:answer/id #uuid "f7d312b2-66ec-592d-821c-8f851491b42f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/cc/cc35ab376bbc7156418f199895c2ff636e46b0955365ac6636156eb901075aa3.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419022}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4b79f667-cd57-53d0-854e-fda5aee2821d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/cd/cd9e524895740aa64ee6143d3d600d6be38b7e6e71b00d402ee249c11c3da36e.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419019} :answer-field/id #uuid "b3d1d66b-023d-5750-9175-e0bf093e91da" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419023}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c266a8db-0d0c-5c54-9052-48c9757f64e6" :question/math-academy-id "q-213260" :question/problem "Suppose we have the set $A=\\{1,2,3,4\\}.$ Which of the following mapping diagrams represents the following relation? \n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y)\\in A\\times A\\,:\\,x<y\\}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The ordered pairs belonging to our relation correspond to the arrows on the mapping diagram. Each arrow starts at the first component of the pair and ends at its second component.\n\nWe're given the following set:\n\n$$\nA=\\{1,2,3,4\\}.\n$$\n\nThe relation $R$ is the set of ordered pairs $(x,y)\\in A\\times A$ such that $x<y.$\n\n- If $x=1,$ then the values $y=2,3,4$ satisfy $x<y.$ So, $R$ contains the ordered pairs $(1,2),\\,(1,3),$ and $(1,4),$ and we should add the arrows $1\\to 2,\\,1\\to 3,$ and $1\\to 4$ to the mapping diagram.\n\n- If $x=2,$ then the values $y=3,4$ satisfy $x<y.$ So, $R$ contains the ordered pairs $(2,3)$ and $(2,4),$ and we should add the arrows $2\\to 3$ and $2\\to 4$ to the mapping diagram.\n\n- If $x=3,$ then the value $y=4$ satisfies $x<y.$ So, $R$ contains the ordered pair $(3,4),$ and we should add the arrow $3\\to 4$ to the mapping diagram.\n\n- If $x=4$ there are no values $y\\in A$ such that $x<y.$ Therefore we do not add arrows starting from $4$ to the mapping diagram.\n\nTherefore, we have the following plot:\n\n![](images/cd/cd9e524895740aa64ee6143d3d600d6be38b7e6e71b00d402ee249c11c3da36e.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186416064 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203920 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "65e9c4fd-dae7-5858-b008-ca0e144fa64a" :knowledge-point/title "Representing Binary Relations Using Digraphs"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "229aefd2-11b3-5163-b906-bbd86d424316" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415996} {:answer/id #uuid "6bce8261-f995-5f77-9c0b-b77b795f8640" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416014} {:answer/id #uuid "781adc97-4ef9-50db-8150-4fb68c77d192" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416015} {:answer/id #uuid "e8456360-6cf3-5dbc-9b03-efff334fe8ec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416036} {:answer/id #uuid "fd84b5df-a423-5a5d-ad6d-2ba9d4d34b30" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416043}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "229aefd2-11b3-5163-b906-bbd86d424316" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415996} :answer-field/id #uuid "16e4a9b9-ed3e-5143-9ca1-42dbee28c3b0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416048}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "538c45af-5de6-56ca-85b6-7f902af26d1f" :question/math-academy-id "q-213261" :question/problem "![](images/de/ded4e8a94730585809c5534fee8d401481c919d529a67cd088e96b8797f2b237.png)\n\nThe directed graph of a relation defined on the set $\\{2,5,8\\}$ is given above. Which of the following ordered pairs belong to the relation?\n\nI. $(2,2)$\n\nII. $(2,8)$\n\nIII. $(8,5)$" :question/worked-solution "A binary relation can be visualized using a directed graph (digraph), where \n\n- the nodes (vertices) of the graph are elements of the underlying set,\n\n- each pair $(x,y)$ from the relation defines an arrow from node $x$ to node $y$,\n\n- each pair $(x,x)$ from the relation defines a loop from node $x$ to itself.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given ordered pairs.\n\n- The pair $(2,2)$ belongs to the relation. Indeed, the directed graph has a loop from the node $2$ to itself.\n\n- The pair $(2,8)$ does not belong to the relation since the directed graph does not have an arrow that starts at the node $2$ and ends at the node $8.$\n\n- The pair $(8,5)$ belongs to the relation. Indeed, the directed graph has an arrow that starts at the node $8$ and ends at the node $5.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186416067 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203920 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "65e9c4fd-dae7-5858-b008-ca0e144fa64a" :knowledge-point/title "Representing Binary Relations Using Digraphs"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1997f0c1-dbf6-5676-9a65-8a59d13b359b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415994} {:answer/id #uuid "4f900224-55b2-5719-9556-cfc3c22bf31d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416006} {:answer/id #uuid "cc702308-7c8f-52c1-8bba-64531db6e716" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416030} {:answer/id #uuid "e38ff878-929d-5ee9-9b71-f303cbb883cc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416034} {:answer/id #uuid "e84d09a8-9f4e-5918-a25a-eab0fa6840b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416037}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1997f0c1-dbf6-5676-9a65-8a59d13b359b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415994} :answer-field/id #uuid "0bc10d4e-de6c-5af8-9374-56bcd531b20c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416047}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8ba430a9-b9f3-5dc8-a610-c4d7d7129920" :question/math-academy-id "q-213262" :question/problem "![](images/4b/4b23d63f453246fb606969ba651104097fca785324d5d0fd2203e176dda19fa5.png)\n\nThe directed graph of a relation defined on the set $\\{\\alpha ,\\beta ,\\gamma \\}$ is given above. Which of the following ordered pairs belong to the relation?\n\nI. $(\\beta ,\\gamma )$\n\nII. $(\\gamma ,\\alpha )$\n\nIII. $(\\alpha ,\\beta )$" :question/worked-solution "A binary relation can be visualized using a directed graph (digraph), where \n\n- the nodes (vertices) of the graph are elements of the underlying set,\n\n- each pair $(x,y)$ from the relation defines an arrow from node $x$ to node $y$,\n\n- each pair $(x,x)$ from the relation defines a loop from node $x$ to itself.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given ordered pairs.\n\n- The pair  $(\\beta ,\\gamma )$ does not belong to the relation since the directed graph does not have an arrow that starts at the node $\\beta $ and ends at the node $\\gamma .$\n\n- The pair $(\\gamma ,\\alpha )$ does not belong to the relation since the directed graph does not have an arrow that starts at the node $\\gamma $ and ends at the node $\\alpha .$\n\n- The pair $(\\alpha ,\\beta )$ belongs to the relation. Indeed, the directed graph has a an arrow that starts at the node $\\alpha $ and ends at the node $\\beta .$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186416063 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203920 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "65e9c4fd-dae7-5858-b008-ca0e144fa64a" :knowledge-point/title "Representing Binary Relations Using Digraphs"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "05f6ce38-4ec7-5c4b-9a30-15cd62314ac4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/60/6048ff5e6150a98c1074716b6d58905171a9d074859b071b43d4795a841bca84.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415985} {:answer/id #uuid "0aca0654-5a0b-5d1a-9c1a-4a631fc7caaf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/d1/d1afa34c5eb553358d3c267cc5a38ed05d8e8566af2d650db714d0d5a9cdcb10.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415986} {:answer/id #uuid "52c5ec6a-04eb-55d2-b596-1ab172faa1b1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/2a/2a0b3b63880acd92b9a549f19b04fc24f14c5c133d6bed4dec23797df5ba4773.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416008} {:answer/id #uuid "7f2ec819-24ad-5575-897b-3d71478af35f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/5c/5c2794b26dd4e70b60e99a77703c5e117793b86dc2c68045620d1619cf6509a4.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416017} {:answer/id #uuid "ee709334-bc56-57b5-87bc-4b39d37c191e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/e5/e5f595bac27c15ffd3adca91b9a20cefdda9abb18123d706fc50d0fc625f5454.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416040}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ee709334-bc56-57b5-87bc-4b39d37c191e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/e5/e5f595bac27c15ffd3adca91b9a20cefdda9abb18123d706fc50d0fc625f5454.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416040} :answer-field/id #uuid "3780fa3c-bf58-5339-9cc7-3a9993d61a13" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416051}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "480a0347-e790-59c1-bcd0-69556d5de562" :question/math-academy-id "q-213265" :question/problem "Let $A=\\{1,2,4\\}.$ Which of the following directed graphs represents the following relation? \n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y)\\in {A}^{2}\\,:\\,y\\le {x}^{2}\\}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "A binary relation can be visualized using a directed graph (digraph), where \n\n- the nodes (vertices) of the graph are elements of the underlying set,\n\n- each pair $(x,y)$ from the relation defines an arrow from node $x$ to node $y$,\n\n- each pair $(x,x)$ from the relation defines a loop from node $x$ to itself.\n\nIn our case, the nodes of the directed graph are marked by the elements of $A.$\n\n![](images/fe/fe1e7ad055ab47eca3234dfc3797a7316c554868a62cb4e46a303d369baeeaa0.png)\n\n \n\nThe relation $R$ is the set of ordered pairs $(x,y)\\in {A}^{2}$ with $y\\le {x}^{2}.$\n\n- If $x=1,$ then the value $y=1$ satisfies $y\\le {x}^{2}.$ So, $R$ contains the ordered pair $(1,1),$ and we should add the following arrow to our directed graph:\n\n$$\n1\\to 1\n$$\n\n- If $x=2,$ then the values $y=1,2,4$ satisfy $y\\le {x}^{2}.$ So, $R$ contains the ordered pairs $(2,1),\\,(2,2),\\,(2,4),$ and we should add the following arrows to our directed graph:\n\n$$\n2\\to 1,\\,2\\to 2,\\,2\\to 4\n$$\n\n- If $x=4,$ then the values $y=1,2,4$ satisfy $y\\le {x}^{2}.$ So, $R$ contains the ordered pairs $(4,1),$ $(4,2),$ $(4,4),$ and we should add the following arrows to our directed graph:\n\n$$\n4\\to 1,\\,4\\to 2,\\,4\\to 4\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following digraph:\n\n![](images/e5/e5f595bac27c15ffd3adca91b9a20cefdda9abb18123d706fc50d0fc625f5454.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186416069 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203918 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "08140eb8-479d-5827-9dee-d51befd7aadd" :knowledge-point/title "Representing Binary Relations Using Mapping Diagrams"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7cda6b40-1607-515b-8d40-b4dc4beca41d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/10/10b6bbee5d698da7aa693c74239a962d777278d25e218b86d59c3a8c274b4bb9.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416016} {:answer/id #uuid "9d73ac0f-098c-56bd-9203-b3e1fcb646a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/21/2105c1e8674eb619a44a5924b0769aacd02ae6de001cddc5e14a326c5e4b0f82.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416021} {:answer/id #uuid "b4ce922a-9354-5a5a-a57f-f0093ed152b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/56/56bce6c8077ecf45d61d6a004eb428fc43f8ffec13218ab020970bcc93c00853.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416027} {:answer/id #uuid "f1473175-ca26-5902-b122-cd8210ebd180" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/13/13d1aefcd491c1d37d2b998aa67515c012aa354feccdd72edc5f6458be22b5ac.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416041} {:answer/id #uuid "fe1c3d01-681a-5cb0-9c34-a465bee5cde6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/c2/c218c7a0015ad9a3b64b96bd41f42cb6fdca268d23192502bd8eec381861c866.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416044}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fe1c3d01-681a-5cb0-9c34-a465bee5cde6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/c2/c218c7a0015ad9a3b64b96bd41f42cb6fdca268d23192502bd8eec381861c866.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416044} :answer-field/id #uuid "bd766538-867d-5ca9-a328-d9671da8903c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416056}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8c72bf3c-0135-57d0-9bad-2176a6cb3b90" :question/math-academy-id "q-213269" :question/problem "Suppose we have the sets $A$ and $B,$ given by\n\n$$\nA=\\{7,8,9,10\\},\\,B=\\{0,1,2,3\\}.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following mapping diagrams represents the following relation? \n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y)\\in A\\times B\\,:\\,x≡y\\,(\\text{mod}\\,3)\\}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The ordered pairs belonging to our relation correspond to the arrows on the mapping diagram. Each arrow starts at the first component of the pair and ends at its second component.\n\nWe're given the following sets:\n\n$$\nA=\\{7,8,9,10\\},\\,B=\\{0,1,2,3,4\\}.\n$$\n\nThe relation $R$ is the set of ordered pairs $(x,y)\\in A\\times B$ with $x≡y\\,(\\text{mod}\\,3).$\n\n- If $x=7,$ then the value $y=1$ satisfies $x≡y\\,(\\text{mod}\\,3).$ So, $R$ contains the ordered pair $(7,1),$ and we should add the arrow $7\\to 1$ to the mapping diagram.\n\n- If $x=8,$ then the value $y=2$ satisfies $x≡y\\,(\\text{mod}\\,3).$ So, $R$ contains the ordered pair $(8,2),$ and we should add the arrow $8\\to 2$ to the mapping diagram.\n\n- If $x=9,$ then the values $y=0,3$ satisfy $x≡y\\,(\\text{mod}\\,3).$ So, $R$ contains the ordered pairs $(9,0)$ and $(9,3),$ and we should add the arrows $9\\to 0$ and $9\\to 3$ to the mapping diagram.\n\n- If $x=10,$ then the value $y=1$ satisfies $x≡y\\,(\\text{mod}\\,3).$ So, $R$ contains the ordered pair $(10,1),$ and we should add the arrow $10\\to 1$ to the mapping diagram.\n\nTherefore, we have the following plot:\n\n![](images/c2/c218c7a0015ad9a3b64b96bd41f42cb6fdca268d23192502bd8eec381861c866.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186416071 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203920 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "65e9c4fd-dae7-5858-b008-ca0e144fa64a" :knowledge-point/title "Representing Binary Relations Using Digraphs"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0ecde3ed-974d-55f9-b20d-3aefeefd4a9d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/f8/f8abac994a863447da2182e86df34cbdbcf019d58c9a606f507403f15890947d.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415990} {:answer/id #uuid "107d4507-8231-5b8c-8e97-84b83cc2d7ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/0a/0a5edb58642893336967966154abdb816c60319743e3b826e5898798298f1bfd.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415991} {:answer/id #uuid "3e31cd7c-7470-5588-9360-d669280cc5a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/47/4779a070a7801248e4fd9ea5e4361b2ae4bff82e5cf7aa372989b84b3eb92c92.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416003} {:answer/id #uuid "b42a2495-1c67-5e03-997f-625dd59d55ce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/17/17bd7e9f3c66b92d036089640bc3b74d5996de9a498bd64f2262a54695e130b4.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416025} {:answer/id #uuid "e393cf06-a753-55ca-b724-8f861d35ce36" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/74/74f2f01dd9b2672f933118f6cec0bc0b83b9a0fa94b0e75536a7db9f0fe34e6b.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416035}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e393cf06-a753-55ca-b724-8f861d35ce36" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/74/74f2f01dd9b2672f933118f6cec0bc0b83b9a0fa94b0e75536a7db9f0fe34e6b.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416035} :answer-field/id #uuid "00feca98-0fa4-5498-b814-25e2c4cd6245" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186416046}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b72e4164-0cff-5d5d-b282-9eeac80bb3aa" :question/math-academy-id "q-213272" :question/problem "Let $A=\\{Z,Q,R\\}.$ Which of the following directed graphs represents the following relation? \n\n$$\nR=\\{(X,Y)\\in {A}^{2}\\,:\\,Y⊆X\\}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "A binary relation can be visualized using a directed graph (digraph), where \n\n- the nodes (vertices) of the graph are elements of the underlying set,\n\n- each pair $(x,y)$ from the relation defines an arrow from node $x$ to node $y$,\n\n- each pair $(x,x)$ from the relation defines a loop from node $x$ to itself.\n\nIn our case, the nodes of the directed graph are marked by the elements of $A.$\n\n![](images/98/983be4b904bbbf72f896c2e96d3691c74179c9e078494d581fb8c9b2c0c64b7f.png)\n\n \n\nThe relation $R$ is the set of ordered pairs $(X,Y)\\in {A}^{2}$ with $Y⊂X.$\n\n- If $X=Z,$ then the value $Y=Z$ satisfies $Y⊆X.$ So, $R$ contains the ordered pair $(Z,Z),$ and we should add the following arrow  to our directed graph:\n\n$$\nZ\\to Z\n$$\n\n- If $X=Q,$ then the values $Y=Z,Q$ satisfy $Y⊆X.$ So, $R$ contains the ordered pairs $(Q,Z),(Q,Q),$ and we should add the following arrows to our directed graph:\n\n$$\nQ\\to Z,\\,Q\\to Q\n$$\n\n- If $X=R,$ then the values $y=Z,Q,R$ satisfy $Y⊆X.$ So, $R$ contains the ordered pairs $(R,Z),$ $(R,Q),$ $(R,R),$ and we should add the following arrows to our directed graph:\n\n$$\nR\\to Z,\\,R\\to Q,\\,R\\to R\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following digraph:\n\n![](images/74/74f2f01dd9b2672f933118f6cec0bc0b83b9a0fa94b0e75536a7db9f0fe34e6b.png)"}]}
